  "articleSummary": "This article argues that the agentic AI threat is not a single sci-fi breakthrough but two ordinary, compounding curves: how long one agent can run autonomously (endurance, citing METR's doubling-time data) and how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate (breadth, citing Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign run through Claude Code). Its key security point is that attackers using stolen cloud credentials (dubbed \"LLMjacking\" by Sysdig) reroute inference costs onto victims, so price and access gates that filter paying customers fail against tenants with stolen keys, and that cheap, repeated attempts make per-attempt defense rates misleading. The tone is an opinionated analytical essay with a sardonic, pop-culture register, framed by a recurring Terminator motif (Kyle Reese's \"absolutely will not stop\" line, \"the robot is an API call,\" \"murder chassis and a leather jacket\") and supported by simple formulas and concept diagrams. The intended audience is engineering and security teams, particularly those who dismiss agentic AI threats as hype, and the translation should preserve the Terminator allusions, the technical terms (LLMjacking, orchestrator, sub-agent, time-horizon), and the terse, punchy sentence rhythm.",
